<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.10.4: Comparing Graphs and Statements that Represent Functions without a Context</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01030</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.10.4: Comparing Graphs and Statements that Represent Functions without a Context</md:title>
    <md:uuid>21083f62-2769-4b2f-a20c-84758a8833bf</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>Here are graphs that represent two <term class="glossary-tooltip">functions</term>, \(f\) and \(g\).</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="A graph. ">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_09_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Decide which function value is greater for each given input. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.
        
       </para>
          <para>Which is greater, \(f(2)\) or \(g(2)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(g(2)\) is greater.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Which is greater, \(f(4)\) or \(g(4)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>They appear to be equal.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Which is greater, \(f(6)\) or \(g(6)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(6)\) is greater.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Which is greater, \(f(8)\) or \(g(8)\)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(8)\) is greater.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Is there a value of \(x\) at which the <term class="glossary-tooltip"> equation</term> \(f(x)=g(x)\) is true? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answers:</para>
          <para>Yes. For example: If the graphs intersect when \(x\) is 4 and when it is 9, then \(f(4)=g(4)\) and \(f(9)=g(9)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Identify at least two values of \(x\) at which the inequality \(f(x)&lt;g(x)\) is true.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answers:</para>
          <para>1, 3, 10, 11, or other \(x\)-values that are less than 4 and greater than 9</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
    <note class="self-check">
      <para>Which of the following is true about the graphs of \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\) below?</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="GRAPH OF TWO FUNCTIONS. ONE IS A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP. THE OTHER IS A LINE WITH A NEGATIVE SLOPE. THE FUNCTIONS INTERSECT AT THE POINTS (1, −3) AND (−2, 0).&#10;">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_10_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
    </note>
    <section>
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section>
      <title>Using Graphs to Find Values</title>
      <para>Two functions, \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\), are graphed below.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_11_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <para>What is the value of \(f(-1)\)?</para>
      <para>Look at where \(x=-1\) touches the graph of \(f\). The \(y\)-value is –3.</para>
      <para>\(f(-1)=-3\)</para>
      <para>What is the value of \(g(-1)\)?</para>
      <para>Look where \(x=-1\) touches the graph of \(g\). The \(y\)-value is –1.</para>
      <para>\(g(-1)=-1\)</para>
      <para>Which value is greater?</para>
      <para>\(g(-1)&gt;f(-1)\)</para>
      <note class="try look-at-me"/>
  <title>Try It: Using Graphs to Find Values</title>
      <para>Two functions, \(f(x)\) and \(g(x)\), are graphed below.</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_13_11_02.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Which is greater, \(f(1)\) or \(g(1)\)?</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.
   </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Here is how to determine which value is greater:</para>
            <para>Looking at the graph, \(f(1)\) is where \(x=1\) reaches the graph of \(f(x)\). The \(y\)-value there is 4. So, \(f(1)=4\).</para>
            <para>Similarly, \(g(1)\) is where \(x=1\) reaches the graph of \(g(x)\). The \(y\)-value there is 2.</para>
            <para>So, \(f(1) &gt;g(1)\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
    </section>
    
  </content>
